<meta>
  title: Quotarelay
  description: Local-first context compression for coding agents, with a free Apache-2.0 core and planned paid team coordination.
  keywords: quotarelay, mcp, coding agents, context compression, local first, token savings
  aiSummary: Quotarelay is a local-first MCP context engine that assembles bounded, explainable context packets for coding agents while keeping repository contents local by default.
  aiKeywords: mcp, context-engine, local-first, coding-agents, privacy, open-core
</meta>

<route>
  path: /
</route>

<ai>
  summary: Public product homepage for Quotarelay. Keep claims limited to shipped local behavior and clearly distinguish planned hosted team features.
  audience:
    - solo developers
    - engineering teams
    - AI coding tool users
  privacy_boundary: Source code, raw context packets, local caches, and .quotarelay state stay local by default.
  monetization: Free Apache-2.0 local core with paid hosted/team/enterprise coordination planned.
  tags:
    - public-site
    - local-first
    - mcp
    - context-compression
    - privacy
</ai>

<template>
  <main class="public-site">
    <section class="public-hero">
      <div class="public-hero__copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Local-first MCP context engine</p>
        <h1>Quotarelay</h1>
        <p class="public-lede">
          Give coding agents the smallest useful packet: relevant code, durable decisions, validation commands,
          cache state, stale-state warnings, and clear omission reasons without uploading your repository by default.
        </p>
        <div class="public-actions">
          <a class="button button--primary" href="https://github.com/quotarelay/quotarelay">GitHub</a>
          <a class="button button--secondary" href="/control-plane">Local control plane</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="signal-board" aria-label="Quotarelay packet proof summary">
        <div class="signal-board__header">
          <span>Context packet</span>
          <strong>bounded</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="signal-row">
          <span>Raw bytes considered</span>
          <strong>24,106</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="signal-row">
          <span>Packet bytes</span>
          <strong>1,361</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="signal-row">
          <span>Local reduction</span>
          <strong>94.35%</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="signal-row signal-row--accent">
          <span>Repeat retrieval</span>
          <strong>cache hit</strong>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="public-band">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">What ships free</p>
        <h2>Useful local core, not a crippled demo.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature-card">
          <h3>Bounded retrieval</h3>
          <p>Exact, overview, task, and diff-aware modes return limited context with typed inclusion and omission reasons.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <h3>Memory and handoff</h3>
          <p>Local decision and guardrail memory can travel with handoff packets, validation suggestions, and blockers.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <h3>Savings proof</h3>
          <p>Local demos report raw bytes, packet bytes, approximate tokens, reduction ratio, cache state, and stale state.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <h3>Privacy boundary</h3>
          <p>Repository contents, raw packets, local caches, and .quotarelay state stay on your machine unless you opt in later.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="public-band public-band--split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Teams and companies</p>
        <h2>Paid value belongs in coordination, governance, and support.</h2>
        <p class="public-copy">
          Quotarelay should be easy to adopt as an open-source local tool. The commercial layer is planned around
          hosted team management, shared policy profiles, aggregate savings reports, auditability, integrations,
          private deployment guidance, SSO, onboarding, and support.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="team-list">
        <li>Organization login and team membership</li>
        <li>Shared guardrails and validation recipes</li>
        <li>Aggregate savings and cache-reuse reporting</li>
        <li>Audit logs, SSO, compliance docs, and private deployment</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
</template>
